00:19:29,100 --> 00:19:30,100
Thank you.

280
00:19:30,100 --> 00:19:31,100
Thank you ma'am.

281
00:19:31,100 --> 00:19:32,100
Absolutely.

282
00:19:32,100 --> 00:19:37,100
I'm going to take this off just so my voice will come a little clearer and we'll see if I can drive this

283
00:19:37,100 --> 00:19:41,100
mouse and get to where I need to be.

284
00:19:41,100 --> 00:19:45,100
Thank you to the mayor and the council for having us.

285
00:19:45,100 --> 00:19:52,100
We are so excited to have this chance to share some extraordinary work that's been happening from the

286
00:19:52,100 --> 00:19:54,100
beginning.

287
00:19:54,100 --> 00:19:55,100
There we go.

288
00:19:55,100 --> 00:19:57,100
How about that?

289
00:19:57,100 --> 00:20:04,100
So many of you may know the logo of United Way is the best known logo in the country in terms of

290
00:20:04,100 --> 00:20:05,100
nonprofits.

291
00:20:05,100 --> 00:20:11,100
But you may not know the logo of our partner in this great work which is Crisis Services of North

292
00:20:11,100 --> 00:20:12,100
Alabama.

293
00:20:12,100 --> 00:20:17,100
I could talk a whole different presentation about them and all the great work that they do.

294
00:20:17,100 --> 00:20:23,100
But United Way is so honored to have them come together with us to offer 211.

295
00:20:23,100 --> 00:20:28,100
And I expect everybody at the podium knows what that is in general.

296
00:20:28,100 --> 00:20:35,100
And I know that Ms. Francis Ackridge does because she has been through our 211 ambassador training.

297
00:20:35,100 --> 00:20:43,100
But I do have, so you guys tell me if I need to go through a little more explanation of what it is in a few

298
00:20:43,100 --> 00:20:44,100
minutes.

299
00:20:44,100 --> 00:20:49,100
But we do always want to start any conversation that we have from United Way saying thank you to the

300
00:20:49,100 --> 00:20:50,100
community.

301
00:20:50,100 --> 00:20:55,100
Thank you to this group of wonderful folks who have said yes, we know the community is growing.

302
00:20:55,100 --> 00:21:01,100
We know that needs are growing as they do in any community that is getting larger.

303
00:21:01,100 --> 00:21:05,100
And we've also had that little thing of the pandemic come at us.

304
00:21:05,100 --> 00:21:12,100
And so it was just time for us to come together and spend that time together investing in this very

305
00:21:13,100 --> 00:21:20,100
important piece of how we keep the quality of life where it is here in Madison County.

306
00:21:20,100 --> 00:21:22,100
So we're very appreciative of that.

307
00:21:22,100 --> 00:21:26,100
So do you guys want a little tutorial about 211?

308
00:21:26,100 --> 00:21:28,100
I can do that really quick.

309
00:21:28,100 --> 00:21:30,100
I'm probably not going to spend a lot of time on these slides.

310
00:21:30,100 --> 00:21:37,100
But for those in the audience maybe that do not know what 211 is, it is a free and confidential service.

311
00:21:38,100 --> 00:21:44,100
And the simplest way I can say is 365 days a year you can call those three numbers from anywhere in the

312
00:21:44,100 --> 00:21:50,100
United States and you can get connected with a caring cost specialist who will say how can we help?

313
00:21:50,100 --> 00:21:57,100
And then they will go in an extensive and comprehensive source of information about local resources,

314
00:21:57,100 --> 00:22:06,100
all of our health and human service nonprofits, many of our agencies and organizations like DHR and

315
00:22:06,100 --> 00:22:09,100
Wellstone are also listed services there.

316
00:22:09,100 --> 00:22:15,100
And we often find that we encounter people in the community that come up to us as individuals and say,

317
00:22:15,100 --> 00:22:19,100
I have this problem and they confide in you, but you don't know the answer.

318
00:22:19,100 --> 00:22:23,100
And the answer always for that is to start by calling 211.

319
00:22:23,100 --> 00:22:27,100
So it's very, a very, very simple thing to do.

320
00:22:27,100 --> 00:22:31,100
And we're so proud as United Way's across the country that 90,

321
00:22:31,100 --> 00:22:36,100
almost 95% of the country is covered by the 211 network.

322
00:22:36,100 --> 00:22:41,100
And we can be proud that Alabama, Tennessee, Mississippi and South Carolina all have 100% coverage.

323
00:22:41,100 --> 00:22:49,100
That means you can dial that number from any landline or cell phone in these states and all those others that are dark blue.

324
00:22:49,100 --> 00:22:54,100
And you can get that call specialist that can be that lifeline to start to connect to work.

325
00:22:54,100 --> 00:22:58,100
And so we're very proud as a system for how that is.

326
00:22:58,100 --> 00:23:00,100
So there are really three ways.

327
00:23:00,100 --> 00:23:03,100
The traditional way has always been to dial those three numbers.

328
00:23:03,100 --> 00:23:05,100
But now we also have a text number.

329
00:23:05,100 --> 00:23:07,100
We're very excited about that.

330
00:23:07,100 --> 00:23:11,100
TXT 211 or 898-211.

331
00:23:11,100 --> 00:23:15,100
People can text their zip code so they get to their local call center.

332
00:23:15,100 --> 00:23:17,100
And they also can visit in Alabama.

333
00:23:17,100 --> 00:23:22,100
Part of our interconnected network online is 211 connectsalabama.org.

334
00:23:22,100 --> 00:23:24,100
So that makes it all toll free.

335
00:23:24,100 --> 00:23:29,100
And what we found in national studies tell us that it takes an individual,

336
00:23:29,100 --> 00:23:36,100
even with Google and with Facebook friends and all those other ways that we have to connect and find help,

337
00:23:36,100 --> 00:23:46,100
that it typically takes five or six calls, six or seven calls, I think, actually, to reach out only to find that what we thought was the answer is not the answer.

338
00:23:46,100 --> 00:23:54,100
And so 211 cuts that right to the quick and gets folks to the resources that are available in our local community.

339
00:23:54,100 --> 00:23:58,100
And I will just give a shout out again for 211.

340
00:23:58,100 --> 00:24:02,100
Our board at United Way back in 2003 was very smart.

341
00:24:02,100 --> 00:24:05,100
They had the chance to bring 211 here.

342
00:24:05,100 --> 00:24:07,100
We could have set up a separate call center.

343
00:24:07,100 --> 00:24:09,100
But we already had helpline.

344
00:24:09,100 --> 00:24:13,100
Many of you guys may know for years here at Crisis Services.

345
00:24:13,100 --> 00:24:17,100
And so that board very wisely said, why would we reinvent the wheel?

346
00:24:17,100 --> 00:24:24,100
Why don't we use the existing call center and expand it and have a partnership with Crisis Services?

347
00:24:24,100 --> 00:24:26,100
And to this day, that's what we do.

348
00:24:26,100 --> 00:24:33,100
And it's been such a good model that five United Ways now do that in North Alabama through Crisis Services.

349
00:24:33,100 --> 00:24:42,100
So we really are a regional 211 call center serving Marshall, Morgan, Limestone, Jackson and Madison.

350
00:24:42,100 --> 00:24:45,100
So that's a good piece to know.

351
00:24:45,100 --> 00:24:48,100
So I call it an infrastructure piece.

352
00:24:48,100 --> 00:24:53,100
I was thinking today, what would this community be like if we didn't have this?

353
00:24:53,100 --> 00:24:59,100
I can't even imagine how frustrated people that we have a lot of mental health stress going on right now.

354
00:24:59,100 --> 00:25:01,100
It'd be at a whole other level.

355
00:25:01,100 --> 00:25:03,100
We'd have more people on the street.

356
00:25:03,100 --> 00:25:05,100
We'd have more people hungry.

357
00:25:05,100 --> 00:25:08,100
We'd have more people looking for mental health services that didn't know where to turn.

358
00:25:08,100 --> 00:25:10,100
We'd have more children abused.

359
00:25:10,100 --> 00:25:13,100
We'd have lots of things if this lifeline were not there.

360
00:25:13,100 --> 00:25:21,100
And I love the story that a young man who met us and learned about 211, the very next day he went home,

361
00:25:21,100 --> 00:25:25,100
and his sister, who was in high school at the time, was crying.

362
00:25:25,100 --> 00:25:27,100
And he asked her what was wrong.

363
00:25:27,100 --> 00:25:31,100
She said, a friend of mine was abused and she doesn't know what to do.

364
00:25:31,100 --> 00:25:37,100
And because he knew about 211, he could get her connected right away with the National Children's Advocacy Center.

365
00:25:38,100 --> 00:25:40,100
And she could start on her healing path.

366
00:25:40,100 --> 00:25:44,100
And that's just one example of how important that lifeline is.

367
00:25:44,100 --> 00:25:47,100
So are you ready for some big numbers?

368
00:25:47,100 --> 00:25:49,100
I should have had these roll-ins.

369
00:25:49,100 --> 00:25:51,100
They're just having them on screen.

370
00:25:51,100 --> 00:25:55,100
But we always like to look at United Way hard data.

371
00:25:55,100 --> 00:25:57,100
What are we doing?

372
00:25:57,100 --> 00:25:59,100
Are we actually getting results from what we're doing?

373
00:25:59,100 --> 00:26:06,100
So in 2019, which I believe was the first year that we received the funding from the United Way,

374
00:26:06,100 --> 00:26:10,100
the funding from the city, we had around 9,000 inquiries.

375
00:26:10,100 --> 00:26:18,100
And that's our chats, our calls, and our online inquiries for Madison County residents only.

376
00:26:18,100 --> 00:26:27,100
And we realized that at that time, 98% of the phone calls coming in from Madison County were coming out of possible zip codes.

377
00:26:27,100 --> 00:26:30,100
So we said this is an opportunity for a partnership.

378
00:26:30,100 --> 00:26:33,100
And thank, very gratefully, the city said yes.

379
00:26:33,100 --> 00:26:38,100
So in 2020, when we began that pandemic, look at the leap there.

380
00:26:38,100 --> 00:26:43,100
Thirteen thousand two fifty nine was the year total for that.

381
00:26:43,100 --> 00:26:45,100
And then this past year, look at that explosion.

382
00:26:45,100 --> 00:26:47,100
Record numbers for our call center.

383
00:26:47,100 --> 00:26:50,100
Twenty one thousand nine sixty one.

384
00:26:50,100 --> 00:27:02,100
And if I go back to March 16th, when United Way said we've got we've got to go work remote and our call centers went remote with a flip of a button, which was wonderful.

385
00:27:02,100 --> 00:27:07,100
We've answered thirty three thousand inquiries, a little over thirty three thousand inquiries.

386
00:27:07,100 --> 00:27:11,100
So we are just really proud.

387
00:27:11,100 --> 00:27:17,100
And I cannot say enough about the call specialists that are on those calls all the time.

388
00:27:17,100 --> 00:27:19,100
They're also because they're at crisis services.

389
00:27:19,100 --> 00:27:25,100
They're in the same room as the suicide hotline or the helpline.

390
00:27:26,100 --> 00:27:31,100
So they can get callers that really need that really fast right to those resources.

391
00:27:31,100 --> 00:27:34,100
And that's a great benefit to us.

392
00:27:34,100 --> 00:27:40,100
We don't know what twenty twenty two is going to look like, but I can't imagine it's going to go back to 19 numbers.

393
00:27:40,100 --> 00:27:52,100
We're just growing so much here in the county and we're drawing some people that see this beautiful, bright, shiny star that is Huntsville, Madison County.

394
00:27:52,100 --> 00:27:53,100
And they want to come and be a part of it.

395
00:27:53,100 --> 00:27:54,100
Maybe they come low skilled.

396
00:27:54,100 --> 00:27:59,100
Maybe they come because they're running away from abuse or some other terrible situation.

397
00:27:59,100 --> 00:28:04,100
And they're going to need the community's help a little bit to get stable and get settled.

398
00:28:04,100 --> 00:28:09,100
And that's another reason that we're seeing some of the numbers grow.

399
00:28:09,100 --> 00:28:15,100
So I want to give a special shout out to the new program that two one one is helping United Way with.

400
00:28:15,100 --> 00:28:17,100
And that is called Ride United.

401
00:28:18,100 --> 00:28:29,100
We have seen in our needs assessment for probably I don't know, three or four studies that the issue around transportation was becoming more and more chronic.

402
00:28:29,100 --> 00:28:34,100
It's becoming a bigger barrier for people to get to work and to key medical appointments and those kind of things.

403
00:28:34,100 --> 00:28:40,100
And we're so proud and grateful to the mayor and city transit that they recognize that, too.

404
00:28:40,100 --> 00:28:45,100
And so we've stood with them for a long time and said, OK, tell us what you're doing.

405
00:28:45,100 --> 00:28:46,100
How's it growing?

406
00:28:46,100 --> 00:28:47,100
What can we do to help?

407
00:28:47,100 --> 00:28:59,100
And then thanks to Toyota manufacturing here, they came to us and said, we'd like to bring a gap filling program for transportation until the city can have time to grow that infrastructure.

408
00:28:59,100 --> 00:29:00,100
We know that's coming.

409
00:29:00,100 --> 00:29:07,100
We always know that city transit is going to be the way and transit in general, the way to get around our community.

410
00:29:07,100 --> 00:29:11,100
But in the meantime, we have an answer for thousands of people.

411
00:29:11,100 --> 00:29:19,100
And so the calls that come in through two one one, it's a portal for getting that resource just like any other resource.

412
00:29:19,100 --> 00:29:23,100
And so they can schedule about five rides for somebody.

413
00:29:23,100 --> 00:29:30,100
But if they need more than one ride, then they're coming to United Way and getting in a more case management related process with us.

414
00:29:30,100 --> 00:29:36,100
But we've had part of the increase in those numbers in two one one is the ride United program.

415
00:29:36,100 --> 00:29:45,100
And so we've seen over nine thousand rides and we count them one way because some people only take a one way in this past year.

416
00:29:45,100 --> 00:29:54,100
And so that's getting people primarily to employment or pre-employment opportunities or getting them to short term medical needs.

417
00:29:54,100 --> 00:30:04,100
And since we began the program with Toyota back in 2020, January of 2020, we've given over eleven thousand rides.

418
00:30:05,100 --> 00:30:08,100
So that's become a real critical part.

419
00:30:08,100 --> 00:30:31,100
I think I saw Scott Erwin in the back a little bit earlier and I'll give Scott a shout to because the work that the city of Huntsville has done to keep people in their homes and avoid eviction with the CARES Act money is also seen that two one one was a natural fit to be the portal for connecting those folks to how to apply and to the agencies that are helping them with that distribution.

420
00:30:31,100 --> 00:30:35,100
And so that's also something that we've seen in the numbers.

421
00:30:36,100 --> 00:30:41,100
So I'm just really going to quickly tell you about a wonderful resource that's available to any of you.

422
00:30:41,100 --> 00:30:43,100
And I encourage you it's live data.

423
00:30:43,100 --> 00:30:51,100
I encourage you to go to this website any time that you wonder what are people needing in my community.

424
00:30:51,100 --> 00:30:54,100
And I know that all of you have your finger on that pulse.

425
00:30:54,100 --> 00:30:57,100
So this is an example of what you see.

426
00:30:57,100 --> 00:30:59,100
And I'm sorry that it's so small as a screenshot.

427
00:30:59,100 --> 00:31:04,100
But you can go on and you can search for you could filter by Madison County.

428
00:31:04,100 --> 00:31:05,100
And that's what I did here.

429
00:31:05,100 --> 00:31:11,100
And I filtered a custom date of March of 20 to December of 21.

430
00:31:11,100 --> 00:31:14,100
That's where I got that thirty three thousand dollars.

431
00:31:14,100 --> 00:31:16,100
I mean, at thirty three thousand number.

432
00:31:16,100 --> 00:31:19,100
But you can see how it breaks out the categories.

433
00:31:19,100 --> 00:31:26,100
We're coding essentially every call that comes into our call center and across the whole state of Alabama at two one one call centers.

434
00:31:26,100 --> 00:31:27,100
They're coding.

435
00:31:27,100 --> 00:31:30,100
So you can see those big numbers are not surprising.

436
00:31:30,100 --> 00:31:34,100
Transportation assistance is a big set of calls that we're getting.

437
00:31:34,100 --> 00:31:37,100
Food is not as big.

438
00:31:37,100 --> 00:31:42,100
We're doing a good job of feeding a lot of people that are in a fragile situation.

439
00:31:42,100 --> 00:31:47,100
But housing and shelter is big as is utility assistance.

440
00:31:47,100 --> 00:31:49,100
So those are kind of our big, big players.

441
00:31:49,100 --> 00:31:53,100
We saw a leap in that covid health related area.

442
00:31:54,100 --> 00:32:00,100
So the cool thing is this that's on the right is the pop out from housing.

443
00:32:00,100 --> 00:32:09,100
So you can see the subsets of calls for housing and most of our housing is around that rental assistance.

444
00:32:09,100 --> 00:32:18,100
People that have been caught in this economic growth that we have where their landlords are going up on their rent and their income isn't going up.

445
00:32:18,100 --> 00:32:25,100
And so there are some people are newly having to call for assistance that weren't in that situation before to know.

446
00:32:25,100 --> 00:32:30,100
I want everybody on council to know that this resource is there for you to use.

447
00:32:30,100 --> 00:32:34,100
You can sort it by zip code so it can go down to your district level.

448
00:32:34,100 --> 00:32:39,100
You can sort it by you can look at the whole state of Alabama.

449
00:32:39,100 --> 00:32:40,100
You can sort it by call center.

450
00:32:40,100 --> 00:32:42,100
See what we're doing in regional.

451
00:32:42,100 --> 00:32:45,100
They're all different ways to look at that data.

452
00:32:45,100 --> 00:32:47,100
But I did want to share that with you.

453
00:32:47,100 --> 00:32:56,100
And also we have as I mentioned Francis has been through this and we hope maybe all the council members or somebody on their staff will go through this.

454
00:32:56,100 --> 00:33:03,100
But we several years ago realized that as much as United Way talks about two one one in our crisis services folks help us share that news.

455
00:33:03,100 --> 00:33:09,100
We weren't getting to everybody to let everybody know that this is the lifeline to call when you need help.

456
00:33:09,100 --> 00:33:13,100
And so we created an everyday citizen ambassador program.

457
00:33:13,100 --> 00:33:15,100
They go through an hour's worth of training.

458
00:33:15,100 --> 00:33:21,100
We're doing it virtually right now and we're going to have the next one on February 11th which is National 2 1 1 day.

459
00:33:21,100 --> 00:33:30,100
